    <title>George Renninger, the 19th century confectioner is regarded as the original creator of Candy Corn.</title>
    <id>https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/9</id>
    <link rel="alternate" href="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/00009/"/>
    <category term="trivia" label="Trivia" scheme="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/"/>
    <published>2026-10-31T12:00:00Z</published>
    <updated>2026-11-01T12:00:00Z</updated>
    <author><name>Lzon</name></author>
    <content type="html" xml:base="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/00009/">

&lt;p&gt;He originally referred to his classic Autumn treat as ‘Chicken Feed’. It wouldn’t become popularly named Candy Corn until after World War 1, when the American diet began incorporating more corn-derived foods.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As one of its original producers, the &lt;a rel=&quot;noopener external&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jelly_Belly&quot;&gt;Goelitz Candy Company&lt;/a&gt; (now Jelly Belly) helped to popularize the candy. They ran ad campaigns including one in which a bold rooster characture declares himself the “King of the Candy Corn Fields”.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the modern era most Candy Corn is produced by Ferrero under the Brach’s brand. They control roughly 85% of the market. The National Confectioners Association has estimated that about 35 million pounds of Candy Corn are produced each year. They have also declared October 30th to be National Candy Corn Day.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candy_corn&quot;&gt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candy_corn&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://alwaysatreat.com/candy-moments/halloween-central/everything-candy-corn/&quot;&gt;https://alwaysatreat.com/candy-moments/halloween-central/everything-candy-corn/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.thederrick.com/opinion/greene-column-like-it-or-not-candy-corn-withstands-the-test-of-time/article_8d728b97-3ac0-4845-a4ab-17282341bf04.html&quot;&gt;https://www.thederrick.com/opinion/greene-column-like-it-or-not-candy-corn-withstands-the-test-of-time/article_8d728b97-3ac0-4845-a4ab-17282341bf04.html&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</content>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title>The term ‘Computer Bug’ was popularized by programmer Grace Hopper after she removed a moth from a malfunctioning computer.</title>
    <id>https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/8</id>
    <link rel="alternate" href="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/000008/"/>
    <category term="trivia" label="Trivia" scheme="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/"/>
    <published>2026-10-28T12:00:00Z</published>
    <updated>2026-11-01T12:00:00Z</updated>
    <author><name>Lzon</name></author>
    <content type="html" xml:base="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/000008/">

&lt;p&gt;In 1947, acting as a lieutenant junior grade for the US Navy Grace Hopper was assigned to the Bureau of Ships Computation Project.
The project was using the Harvard Mark II computer to perform ballistics calculations among other things. After encountering a
malfunction in the computer Hopper and her team discovered that a moth had gotten stuck on one of the
machine’s relays and was the ultimate cause of the fault. This amusing detail would later be included in her team’s notes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Using &lt;em&gt;‘bug’&lt;/em&gt; to refer to a fault or malfunction in a machine pre-dates Grace Hopper’s use of the word, but she is often credited with
popularizing the word for use by programmers. In modern software development fixing incorrect codes is often called &lt;em&gt;‘debugging’&lt;/em&gt;, and
a tool that helps to identify bad codes is called a &lt;em&gt;‘debugger’&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.vassar.edu/grace-hopper/achievements&quot;&gt;https://www.vassar.edu/grace-hopper/achievements&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grace_Hopper&quot;&gt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grace_Hopper&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_Mark_II&quot;&gt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_Mark_II&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</content>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title>Phineas Gage was a 19th century American man who survived an iron rod piercing his skull.</title>
    <id>https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/7</id>
    <link rel="alternate" href="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/000007/"/>
    <category term="trivia" label="Trivia" scheme="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/"/>
    <published>2026-10-27T12:00:00Z</published>
    <updated>2026-10-27T12:00:00Z</updated>
    <author><name>Lzon</name></author>
    <content type="html" xml:base="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/000007/">

&lt;p&gt;As a young man Gage was a construction worker under contract with the Rutland and Burlington Railroad company. On September 13, 1848 the 25 year old Gage was on the line when his tamping rod was accidentally fired upward by a gunpowder fuse, at very high speed. With his head directly in the rod’s path, it pierced his skull and continued to fly a long distance behind him.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To the surprise of his doctors, Phineas Gage both survived the initial injury and was both coherent and able to speak to them. In a matter of months he was recovered enough to return to work.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;He lived for another 12 years after the incident, and in that time he was reported to have had a significantly changed personality. He had become more impulsive and quick to anger. Eventually he would die of a seizure while working as a stagecoach driver in Chile.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The incident is known as the “American Crowbar Case”, and became the subject of academic research and discussion throughout the 19th century. It posed important questions related to the functioning of the brain, and the nature of consciousness.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phineas_Gage&quot;&gt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phineas_Gage&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.britannica.com/biography/Phineas-Gage&quot;&gt;https://www.britannica.com/biography/Phineas-Gage&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</content>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title>Among all species Humans are one of the best at long-distance endurance running.</title>
    <id>https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/6</id>
    <link rel="alternate" href="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/000006/"/>
    <category term="trivia" label="Trivia" scheme="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/"/>
    <published>2026-10-26T12:00:00Z</published>
    <updated>2026-10-26T12:00:00Z</updated>
    <author><name>Lzon</name></author>
    <content type="html" xml:base="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/000006/">
&lt;p&gt;A noteworthy ability of the Human species is to run for long, uninterrupted periods of time.
Few species are better, and Humans are the only ape that is capable of it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Among many others, specific traits which enable this include:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Taller body with more surface area which facilitates evaporative cooling (sweating)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Wider heel bone to better absorb impact&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Longer legs enabling a greater stride&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It is an ability that likely evolved in conjunction with &lt;a rel=&quot;noopener external&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persistence_hunting&quot;&gt;Persistence Hunting&lt;/a&gt;,
a technique which involves chasing an animal over long distances. The Human hunter can withstand the chase better than their prey, and eventually
they will catch it even if initially the animal can run faster. If the hunter can keep chase long enough, the prey animal will either
succumb to exhaustion, overheating or injury.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endurance_running_hypothesis&quot;&gt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endurance_running_hypothesis&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persistence_hunting&quot;&gt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persistence_hunting&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.ucdavis.edu/blog/humans-are-born-run&quot;&gt;https://www.ucdavis.edu/blog/humans-are-born-run&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</content>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title>The Pygmalion Effect is a psychological phenomemon in which placing high expectations on someone leads
to their better performance.</title>
    <id>https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/5</id>
    <link rel="alternate" href="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/000005/"/>
    <category term="trivia" label="Trivia" scheme="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/"/>
    <published>2026-10-25T12:00:00Z</published>
    <updated>2026-10-25T12:00:00Z</updated>
    <author><name>Lzon</name></author>
    <content type="html" xml:base="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/000005/">
&lt;p&gt;The Effect is an example of a self-fulfilling prophecy, the subject of the expectation performs better because of their belief that their
performance is required, and not merely an optional choice.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Pygmalion Effect is named for the sculptor Pygmalion from greek mythology. In his story he crafts a statue, and falls so intensely in love with his creation
that it transforms into a real woman.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The term was coined by psychologists Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacombson. They conducted a study on school children in which the teacher
expressed both high and low expectations on the students. As measured by IQ test scores, children who were expected to perform well had higher scores.
Conversely, children who were expected to perform poorly by their teachers had lower scores.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://example.org&quot;&gt;https://example.org&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pygmalion_effect&quot;&gt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pygmalion_effect&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pygmalion_(mythology)&quot;&gt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pygmalion_(mythology)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</content>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title>People can be Ambiverted, meaning they are equal parts Introverted and Extroverted.</title>
    <id>https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/4</id>
    <link rel="alternate" href="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/000004/"/>
    <category term="trivia" label="Trivia" scheme="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/"/>
    <published>2026-10-24T12:00:00Z</published>
    <updated>2026-10-24T12:00:00Z</updated>
    <author><name>Lzon</name></author>
    <content type="html" xml:base="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/000004/">
&lt;p&gt;Introverted people obtain gratification from being alone, and consider social engagement to be mentally draining.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Extroverted people are the inverse of introverted, they are gratified and energized by being around others.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ambiversion is a third personality trait at the mid point along a spectrum between introversion and extroversion.
It was first proposed in 1923 by the American psychologist &lt;a rel=&quot;noopener external&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_Smith_Conklin&quot;&gt;Edmund Smith Conklin&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extraversion_and_introversion#Ambiversion&quot;&gt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extraversion_and_introversion#Ambiversion&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</content>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title>The Costco hotdog combo has not had a price increase in over four decades.</title>
    <id>https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/3</id>
    <link rel="alternate" href="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/000003/"/>
    <category term="trivia" label="Trivia" scheme="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/"/>
    <published>2026-10-23T12:00:00Z</published>
    <updated>2026-10-23T12:00:00Z</updated>
    <author><name>Lzon</name></author>
    <content type="html" xml:base="https://lzon.ca/posts/trivia/000003/">
&lt;p&gt;In 1984, Costco began offering a $1.50 combo deal in its food courts which included a soda and a single hot dog.
The deal was introduced only one year after opening its very first warehouse. As of 2026 the price has never been increased on the combo.
If adjusted for inflation the cost of the combo would have risen over three fold (to $4.86 USD at time of writing).
As late as 2024 Costco has stated publically that they do not ever intend to increase the price.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Costco_hot_dog&quot;&gt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Costco_hot_dog&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</content>
  </entry>
